<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Schism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1378">1378</a>–<a href="/years/1417">1417</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Great Western Schism, Papal Schism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Schism was a split in the Catholic Church from 1378 to 1417, when rival popes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and from 1409 a third line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, each claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> with the backing of different kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> ended it by deposing or accepting the resignation of the claimants and electing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> in 1417.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-schism.jpg" alt="Western Schism" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grandes_Chroniques_de_Frances_de_Charles_V_-_BNF_fr2813_f208_(Clercs_se_disputant).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
